#passage(("All of these people, they agree on one thing: there's no one who wouldn't say there is a Creator.", "Now, let's say almost 2000 people, they all agree that David exists. I have a view of David, an idea about David, a perception of David, and let's say the other 2000 people have a different perception of David. David is one person. Just because there's different perceptions of that one person, that does not mean there's a multitude of people. The reality is that someone has a correct perception and someone has a false perception. So if I have the perception of you that you are from India, and another person has a perception of you that you are from Sri Lanka, his perception is wrong and my perception is correct, based on the idea that my information is directly from you.", "So when we have all of these different religions, they agree that there is a higher power. They have different perceptions of God; the question is: which perception is the correct perception? The way to know which perception is the correct perception is to look at that person, the Prophet and the Messenger of that Religion, or that Scripture of that Religion, and see: where is the evidence that is directly from God? If there is evidence that is directly from God, therefore now you know directly from the source what the source is. If there is no evidence that is from God, then I do not have to accept your understanding of God, which is probably a made-up, man-made idea. So the way for you to know what are the attributes of the Creator is through the Religion: investigating that religion and looking what evidence are there that show that this religion is from the Creator.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Why Does God Not Communicate With Humans? Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1c92faSXNec", ((436, "7:16–7:21"), (473, "7:53–8:22"), (508, "8:28–8:57"),), id: 889)
