#passage(("I have a criteria I usually put forth when it comes to investigating religions. And I would say this criteria is very rational, and anybody who's non-religious or a Muslim or other will agree that this makes sense. You know, the Quran refers to Islam as the religion of truth. So if we want to know what the religion of truth is, the first thing is that religion needs to claim it is from God. It needs to claim that it is the absolute truth. If that religion doesn't claim that, then that religion, I don't even need to worry about it. So if I write a book, Harry Potter, why are we not following the religion of Harry Potter? You're going to tell me Harry Potter is not a religion. I'll tell you why: it doesn't claim it's from God. Doesn't claim is the truth. It's a story. So we don't even investigate it, because it doesn't matter.", "But what will surprise you is that so many religions, they don't even claim to be from God. I'll give you an example: Christianity, which is, you can say, the major religion in this country. If you read the New Testament, the writers of their book, which they call the Bible, the New Testament, none of them starts by saying: I'm writing the revelation of God. None of them does that. You have some Christians who [quote] one of the verses from one called apostle Paul, who wasn't even meeting Jesus, came later on, but they still follow him. He says the old scripture is from God, but he was referring to the Old Testament, cuz at his time that New Testament was not yet written in that way. They have gospels: they say the gospel of Mark, Matthew, Luke, and John. You told them: does John say that he's writing the words of God? You get crickets. Nowhere in his entire gospel he says he's writing the word of God. Matthew is the same thing. Luke is the same thing. So if these people didn't even claim that they're writing the revelation from God, why should I believe they were writing the revelation from God?", "So essential criteria is that that religion needs to first even claim that it is from God, and that would disqualify so many religions. It needs to claim that it's the truth.", "You'll go to some Sikhs, they will tell you that they have their own concept of truth. Some philosophers in Hinduism and within Sikism gave this parable of elephant. They will tell you truth is subjective, so it's like an elephant: there's an elephant here; if I'm touching the feet of the elephant, I'm feeling that this is a pillar. Imagine you're blind, you're touching the feet: oh, it's a pillar. Another person is touching the horn of, or the nose of the elephant: they think it's a snake. Another person is touching the ear. So they're essentially saying: this is the truth; everybody is seeing a side of the truth; everybody has their own way to the truth. Everyone is touching the elephant, but you just think different things. So Muslims are okay, according to them. We're fine, nothing to worry about. So if you're a montheist, you truly believe in what you do, you try to be a good person (according to them, good person means just to practice your own faith), you'll be okay.", "So if your religion does not claim is true, and if I don't believe in it or follow it I'm punished, why should I bother? Why should I worry to begin with? I don't need to. I'll just stick to Islam, because Islam makes that claim. Islam is explicitly clear. Allah in the Quran, he says: verily the only religion acceptable in the sight of Allah is Islam. Allah also says: whoever seeks any religion whatsoever other than Islam, it will not be accepted from him, and he will be among the losers in the afterlife. So that is something that Islam explicitly claims. So that's the first criteria: claims to be from God and claims to be the truth.", "The second thing is that religion must be free from contradictions, errors, mistakes. If God wanted to tell you something or reveal something to you, that thing which God reveals to you is not going to contain the thing and its opposite. So God is not going to say to you pray, and in the next verse, don't pray. So all the religions which are false share in contradictions. Allah says in the Quran about the Quran itself: if it was from other than Allah, they would find in it much contradictions, much errors, much mistakes.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “How Do You Know Which Religion Is From God? A Detailed Comprehensive Analysis | The Muslim Lantern”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9F5gS1yWIRI", ((304, "5:04–6:05"), (365, "6:05–7:15"), (439, "7:19–7:30"), (453, "7:33–8:33"), (513, "8:33–9:32"), (572, "9:32–10:39"),), id: 939)
// U ML412:148-170 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("The 3rd thing is inconsistencies, contradictions. In the Old Testament as well, there is. If you check Genesis, it has a lot of contradictions, like about plants and the sun rising, and there were no plants, and then there were plants in the next chapter. You will find contradictions within Genesis itself as well. If there is a contradiction, there is a human interpolation. There are changes that took place to that Scripture, which means I cannot trust it to be from God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “2 Jews Question The Truth of Islam! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kKviK80NYiM", ((348, "5:48–6:28"),), id: 940)
