#passage(("You have a human being, a person; he says he's a Prophet of God: Prophet Muhammad, Jesus, Moses, whoever that Prophet is. Here is the evidence that we can see today: the Prophet is not here today, but we have their Scripture, their evidence, their revelation that came from God, and we have it carbon dated to the 1st century. We know it's not changed or been corrupted. It has been preserved through an oral tradition and written tradition that we have, that we can verify this is the same information the Prophet said. So we have that Scripture. Now we look at that Scripture. We know that Prophet or Messenger existed 1,400 ago.", "So what we do is we take ourselves in a time machine and we go back 1,400 years ago. What did people believe? They believed all sorts of ridiculous things. What was people's knowledge about earth and the universe and medicine and everything? It was many, many incorrect information that is widespread through the Greeks, through the ancient civilisations, through the Chinese, whoever was there. They had many, many, many superstitions and incorrect beliefs. And that person was in the deserts of Arabia 1,400 years ago, Prophet Muhammad. His knowledge, what should it reflect? If he isn't truly a Messenger from God, whatever Scripture he's going to bring is going to reflect that knowledge.", "For instance, an example to give for that: you have the Hindus. You look at the Bhagavad Gita, you look at their Scriptures, it will tell you there is a bull, he has horns, and with his two horns, he's holding planet earth (AtharvaVeda4.11.1). A massive bull: imagine this gigantic bull in space, and he has the planet earth with his two horns. Why? This is what people believed! Thousands of years ago, this is what they thought. They tell you that the sun has seven horses pulling it (RigVeda1.50.8-1.50.1). Why did they say these things? They say these things because these were the beliefs of the time.", "You look at the Bible, for example, and you want