#passage(("So if Constantine accepted Christianity, therefore Christianity is true? How does that follow logically? Jesus didn't go to other nations and spread the gospel. He didn't predict that. He commanded them to do that. So that's a self-fulfilling prophecy: he said, go and preach the religion. By the way, Muslims had the biggest empire in history, and we're talking about within 100 years from prophet Muhammad. You're not going to accept that as evidence. How does that make Christianity true, if you don't say that it makes Islam true?", "The only reason Christianity spread in the Roman empire is because of [Constantine], because of course he's the one who established the council; he was the emperor. So when he accepted the faith, then the subject accepted the faith after him. That's just common sense. But Muslims, who actually did not have Constantine or any of these big major empires, fought against the Roman empire and defeated them. You do not know what the first believers that were going around teaching were teaching. You don't have anything reliable from that. I believe the followers of Jesus was preaching the same thing I believe: one true God worthy of worship. That's not objective evidence, to say that just because religion spread in a particular place in the world, that therefore this religion must be true. That doesn't follow logically.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Catholic Attempts To Disprove Islam Using Catholicism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lzIJWroR7Ec", ((2210, "36:50–38:16"), (2302, "38:22–39:35"),), id: 953)
