#passage(("You said it depends on what you call proof. No, I said to you, proof is objective evidence. I give you examples of eyewitness testimony to validate an historical event. In the case of Islam, I give you examples of proof: carbon dated manuscripts from the time of the event, or very near the time of the event, like the first century, or the time where the events happened; an oral tradition, like I gave you in the Quran, that can be presented and shown even until this day, with traces of historical names of all the people who took the Quran from the Prophet himself, with their names and biographies and all of that. We have this. So I can give you an example of proof, but you wouldn't have it. I give you actually five different examples of categories of proof in Islam, four or five, and you cannot present one in Christianity. So it's not about what you consider proof. There's many things that can be proof. It is the fact that there isn't any proof in Christianity.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Tactically Turns The Tables On A Christian! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=miaGqA6AFCs", ((2054, "34:14–35:03"),), id: 959)
// U ML040:695-736 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("That's not circularity, if the evidence in the Quran can be established objectively outside the Quran. For example: if the Quran makes multiple prophecies of the future, and then we observe these prophecies, then this is information in the Quran, but proves the Quran externally by the events happening outside the Quran. So when we say the Quran proves itself, that's what I mean: the information within it, we can make objective arguments outside of the Quran itself to prove that this must be the speech of God.", "The Quran, regardless of evidence, is historically preserved. So we can rely on it as a historically reliable source. The Bible isn't. So two claims here. I've already established the first one, I would say, from Christian scholarship. But the second one I'm going to make is also that the Bible doesn't have any objective evidence for [its] truth, and you're welcome to present one to me.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Catholic Attempts To Disprove Islam Using Catholicism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lzIJWroR7Ec", ((2093, "34:53–35:25"), (2137, "35:37–35:59"),), id: 960)
// U ML040:794-935 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("Nonverifiable testimonies of believers that say, \"Oh, we saw this, or we saw that\": it's not objective evidence. I cannot see it, cannot test it. Give me a miracle that I can test today. Can you bring me a person who's not healed, and then I can go to someone who I know who can test him and can do MRI scan and can see the sickness, and then we can see him healed? But to say \"just go see them\", you're not [giving] anything we can test.", "We have some concept called [karamat], which is these miraculous acts that happens for righteous Muslims, but none of us uses them as evidence, because I cannot demonstrate it to you right now objectively. Objective is something that is not based on first person experience, that you and me can test out. You and me can agree this is consistent and rational, that I can test out today and verify. If you don't have anything like that, you have nothing objective.", "I'll tell you the miracles I saw. I saw this miracles of this African dude somewhere in Africa who says he has the Holy Spirit and he flies, and there's two ropes tied in his back, and he pretends that he's doing a miracle of flying. I saw that woman in the Christian government. She was going healing people and she was making these noises, this woman that is related to Trump, and people are falling. I'm sorry to say, but this is what we've been seeing. You talk about investigation; this is the investigation I have done. Most people, with all due respect, I'm not saying this to disrespect you, most people look at these things and laugh. They're like: \"Come on, you're not going to mumble something and tell me this is tongues, and I'm speaking tongues, and people are being healed and whatever.\"", "And by the way, I've seen actual examples of liars who've been exposed in many places in the Middle East, where they go to churches, they pretended that they were Muslims. They wear an entire full covering and they go to the church and pretend, and they're exposed later as Christians. So I've seen many, many examples of lies. That's what I've seen. Where is the evidence I can look at?", "How can I verify that those people were not people who are lying to support their belief? You cannot verify this information today. The people are not alive today. And you don't know if it's a forgery, that someone has attributed things to people that haven't happened. Can I see the miracle of Fatima today with my own eyes? Then it's not evident. That's the point that I keep making.", "I can give an atheist another type of epistemological evidence that is not necessarily based on seeing, but it's objective. I'm not going to say to the atheist, for example: Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, multiplied water for the army when he was alive, therefore this is a miracle of Prophet Muhammad (by the way more attested than any miracle you have, cuz we have eyewitness accounts and their names and [biographies] and everything), therefore Islam is the truth. Because he's going to say to me: I haven't seen this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Catholic Attempts To Disprove Islam Using Catholicism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lzIJWroR7Ec", ((2396, "39:56–40:42"), (2455, "40:55–41:48"), (2521, "42:01–42:41"), (2565, "42:45–43:11"), (2649, "44:09–45:11"), (2724, "45:24–46:03"),), id: 961)
