#passage(("You said that you just believe in God, but you're not really following a specific religion. Then you said that you do good. So how do you know what God is and who God is? Personal experience? There are people who worship everything today, including potatoes and rats and cows and everything and trees; people worship everything today. So if you perceive God to be something through your personal experience, and another person perceives him to be something else through his personal experience, how can we determine who's right? They had a personal experience like you did. So they would claim that the cow is actually an infinite being that, in the nighttime, when it's dark, changes into that perfect being and does all of these different things. So, still a personal experience: \"I had a dream where the cow came with wings and fed me some milk\", and this and that.", "The point I'm trying to make is that if you claim you don't follow religion, just based on your own personal experience, that is just a claim that anyone can make, and no one will ever find guidance. We'll end up with anarchy and chaos, everyone claiming that \"This is right and this is wrong\", \"My personal experience is right over yours\", \"My God is better than yours\". That cannot ever be guidance, and there has to be an objective way in which people can come to an agreement in the end.", "The second thing is about the fruits, the good and the bad things that they do. You cannot determine right and wrong without God. If you use your personal experience, everyone has a personal experience, and what you say is right, they will say is wrong, and what you say is wrong, they will say is right. It does not make any sense to have that worldview.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic \"Confused\" By Simple Islamic Concepts?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OLmq6y3PiVo", ((1167, "19:27–21:00"), (1265, "21:05–21:28"), (1295, "21:35–21:51"),), id: 965)
