#passage(("There's something called superstition, and there's a lot of superstitious things that enters in different cultures. For example, China: you've got a lot of superstitions that are related to numbers, for example, number 13, number 7. It depends on the culture; they've got a lot of superstitious beliefs. The thing about Islam is this: the Prophet ﷺ, when he came 1400 years ago, peace be upon him, he forbade people to be superstitious. He negated the superstitious things that were present in his culture. He said they don't exist. For example, the Arabs used to believe that the disease of one animal transmits to the other animal by itself. So then the Prophet said to them: \"how did the first animal get the disease?\", if the disease only spreads from one to another? So the Prophet was against these types of superstitions. The Arabs used to throw a bird. If the bird goes right, then you decide: \"this is a good thing, I will do it\"; if it goes left: \"no, I'm not gonna do that thing\". So, they were very superstitious, and the Prophet forbade them from doing these things. He said that there's a Creator, Controller, that created the laws, that created everything in the universe, and things run within these laws. It's not your superstition what makes you do this or don't do that, or where the bird goes is what you should do. So Islam is against superstition, and Islam brings things which satisfies your heart, satisfies your mind and satisfies your intellect.", "So, Muslims don't just believe because they're born in a specific culture, a specific country, but they actually investigate and look at the evidences in the Quran. And based on these evidences he will know whether this is from God or it's not from God. That's a rational thing to do: to look at different faiths, and to look at what they present, objective evidence, of whether you should accept it or not. Everyone has a culture. Everyone has his own way, everyone has his own tradition.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Polish Student Amazed By Islamic Teachings! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pf1BQGnAXZc", ((223, "3:43–5:04"), (312, "5:12–5:34"),), id: 1015)
