#passage(("Why do I believe that Islam is the Truth? Well, there's multiple different reasons, specifically about why Islam is the Truth, not why we believe in a Creator. These are two separate things. It's a commonality between us and Christians and Jews and Hindus and every other religion that believes in a God, that we believe in a God! So, if I bring evidence that there is a creator, this is just a theistic thing that all Theists will agree upon.", "The Quran and Hadith (Prophetic sayings) talk about the future. There're multiple prophecies of the future that Prophet Muhammadﷺ ('alayhi sSalatu wa sSalam: Peace and blessings be upon Him) made, and the Quran made. The difference between them and biblical prophecies, for example, is that they're not vague. They're clear and specific. Unlike what many Christians, sadly, I see what they do is that they look at the reality and they put it on the verse. No, I'm talking about clear verses or clear prophecies of the future that indicate certain realities.", "Another example is the Quran itself; its miraculous nature, its challenge of inimitability: nobody can bring a scripture like the Quran. Nobody can imitate it. That challenge was there for 1400 years and remains until today. And you don't have to be a Muslim to undergo this challenge. There're Arabic-speaking Jews, Arabic-speaking Christians, Arabic-speaking Atheists and Arabic-speaking professors in Oxford, and those people, none of them have been able to produce something like the Quran, imitate it or bring a chapter like it or a scripture like it.", "Also, the Quran's description of the world, things that are created, natural phenomena: Quran's description of mountains, sky, stars, galaxy, the universe. So it's not the other way around: what we're learning today is trying to keep up with the Quranic knowledge.", "The life of the Prophetﷺ as an example. In this case, we're talking about evidence for Islam, so we're assuming the existence of God. So, if you study hi