#passage(("Of course, there's differences in the laws and in the teachings. For example, at the time of Joseph: Joseph is a messenger of God. That messenger of God, the Quran records that his family prostrated to him, prostrating to a human being. In our tradition, our Sharia, that is not permissible. It wasn't a prostration of worship; it was a prostration of respect. But Allah records that that happened at the time of Joseph. So, in his legislation, in his Sharia, in his law, that was allowed, to prostrate to someone out of respect.", "So if you see a way of worship or a specific law or something that was different, that doesn't go against the teachings of Islam anyways. Even if I were to accept that that was preserved, which is not; if I were to say, \"Okay, that is preserved, no problem, I'm going to accept it the way it is\", even if I did, that doesn't contradict the Islamic narrative, because there's different Sharia for different prophets.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Niqabi Challenges the Continuity of Islam with Previous Scriptures! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7-kSmD09rxI", ((577, "9:37–10:12"), (614, "10:14–10:33"),), id: 1130)
