#passage(("We would say that Prophets of God do not commit Major Sins. They do not commit things which are a bad thing when it comes to character, like lying, fornicating, all of these things which will be an attack on a person's character. And they could do things not in the best way possible, what we may refer to as Minor Sins. So unlike the Creation, Allah will correct them, and they will seek repentance straight away, and they will be forgiven straight away. Why? Because as Prophets of God, if they do something that is not optimal -they are examples for Mankind- it has to be corrected instantly, in order for us not to follow that thing.", "There's an example in the Quran, an incident of Prophet Muhammadﷺ: there was a person who was blind, called Abdullah Ibn Um Maktoom, and he came to Prophet Muhammadﷺ; he wanted him to teach him. Prophetﷺ was speaking with the Heads of Quraish قريش, the Heads of the Bedouin Arabs. So he was concerned about them, because if they believe, all of the people will believe. That's how the people used to be in the time: if the tribe Leader believes, most of the tribe believes. So Prophetﷺ was focusing on them, and then that man, the blind man, came to the Prophetﷺ, and the Prophetﷺ frowned in his face. But he was blind; he didn't see him frowning anyways. He didn't say anything bad to him, he didn't curse him, he just frowned because he wanted something which is good. Then Allahﷻ in the (Quran80:1-3) told him: why did you frown; maybe that person would have gotten purification from you? So then there was a Chapter in the Quran in which all the beginning of the chapter talks about this incident.", "There are other examples for other Prophets of God, like Adam عليه السلام eating from the Tree. It's a big one for Christians, not for Muslims, because in Islam we don't have the Original Sin. For Christians that's all the basis of their religion. For us, it's something that happened, and straight away he was forgiven, and that's it; I have nothing to do with it. But in Christianity that's the basis of their Creed. But that's an example, because Allah says in the (Quran20:121) وَعَصَىٰ آدَمُ رَبَّهُ فَغَوَىٰ (And Adam disobeyed his Lord -when he ate from the Tree-), him and حواء (Eve), both of them.", "So these are examples of this type of things that you might say that were not optimal from the Prophets and Messengers. But still, they were the best of the Creation. There was no one in the Creation who could do better than them. But the ultimate perfection in all Actions and Attributes is to Allah; it is not to the Creation. So we give them their status, but we do not over praise them and we do not under praise them in the same time. Whatever was not optimal that happened, it was corrected straight away. You'll find the trace of it being corrected, whatever example you have in mind.", "If Islam taught that Prophetﷺ was perfect in every way, I would defend it. But the reality is Islam doesn't teach that. So I do not need to teach it. It's not easy to defend this idea, the idea of Jesus, because God is Perfect. When you give the absolute perfection to anything else, you are likening that thing to God; you're also calling it God. So if you want to say that Jesus is perfect in every way, then there cannot be anything that is not Optimal. Because there's perfection now, everything has to be optimal, not just good, but it has to be optimal. That's what Perfection is: perfection is not 80%, which is good; it is Optimal, it is 100%. So you'd have to demonstrate that.", "Perfection can mean different things. We believe that Human Beings are perfect to do that which God created them to do. We believe Prophet Muhammedﷺ was perfect, from that perspective, to fulfill the role that he was asked to do. But that does not mean that there was nothing that you can look at and say that was not Optimum per say, because he was a human being.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Irrational Criteria For God's Existence! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xz7nptUD9Ug", ((1757, "29:17–29:50"), (1804, "30:04–30:57"), (1862, "31:02–31:34"), (1899, "31:39–32:52"), (2000, "33:20–34:00"), (2040, "34:00–34:34"),), id: 1182)
